
=== Part 1: Development with morloc-manager

This is the first of two tutorials covering the morloc-manager workflow.
Part 1 covers installation, environment management, and developing morloc
programs. link:tutorial-deployment.asc[Part 2] covers serving, freezing,
and deploying.

==== Why morloc-manager

Morloc programs combine functions from multiple languages -- Python, R,
{cpp} -- under one type system. The compiler, language runtimes, and
standard library modules all need to coexist in a consistent environment.
`morloc-manager` provides that environment as a named, isolated container
with its own module store, compiler version, and optional system
dependencies.

You work on the host, edit files in your normal editor, and run
`morloc-manager run` to execute commands inside the container. Your
working directory is bind-mounted automatically, so there is no file
copying.

==== Step 0: Prerequisites

Download the `morloc-manager` binary from the
https://github.com/morloc-project/morloc/releases[GitHub releases page].
Binaries are published for x86 Linux, Linux ARM, and macOS. On macOS you
may need to clear the quarantine attribute:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ xattr -d com.apple.quarantine morloc-manager
----

You also need a container engine: Podman (recommended for rootless local
work), Docker, or Apptainer (also known as Singularity, the common engine
on HPC clusters). If you plan to use Apptainer, jump ahead to
<<_step_7_using_apptainer_singularity,Step 7>> after reading this section;
the dnd walkthrough up through Step 6 reads identically under any engine.

If both engines are installed, pick one with `--engine` on your first
`new`; the choice is remembered for later environments:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager new --engine podman
----

`--engine` accepts `docker`, `podman`, `apptainer`, or `singularity`
(the last is an alias for `apptainer`), or `none` for the native backend.

If only one engine is installed, `new` auto-detects it and `--engine` is
optional.

==== Step 1: Create an environment

An *environment* is a named, self-contained Morloc installation: a
container image, a data directory for modules and binaries, and optionally
a custom Dockerfile layer with extra dependencies. Everything in this
tutorial happens inside one.

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager new dnd
Pulling ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:latest...
Created environment: dnd
Initializing morloc (this may take several minutes)...
Environment 'dnd' is ready.
Set 'dnd' as the default environment.
----

This pulls the latest stable release. You can pin a specific version with
`--morloc-version`, e.g. `morloc-manager new dnd --morloc-version 0.81.0`, but
omitting it is the recommended default. The first environment you create becomes
the *default* -- the one every command targets when you do not pass `--env`.

NOTE: `morloc-manager new` runs `morloc init` inside the container as part
of setup, which compiles the shared library, the nexus binary, and the
language bindings. You will see compilation output from gcc/g++, Python
`build_ext`, and possibly a warning like `Skipping Julia setup` -- this is
expected; the environment is still ready.

You can also run the interactive wizard by calling `morloc-manager new`
with no arguments -- it prompts for a name (defaulting to `default`).

Because `dnd` is the first environment, it is already the default, so
subsequent commands target it with no extra step. (To point the default at a
different environment later, run `morloc-manager modify --env <name> --set-default`.)

Verify it:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager info
Default:        dnd
Local engine:   podman
System engine:  unset
SELinux:        not detected

Directories:
  Config (local)       ~/.config/morloc (exists)
  Data (local)         ~/.local/share/morloc (exists)
  Config (system)      /etc/morloc (not found)
  Data (system)        /usr/local/share/morloc (not found)

Local environments:
  dnd [0.85.0] (default)
----

More detailed information is available with `morloc-manager info dnd`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager info dnd
Name:           dnd
Scope:          local
Default:        yes
Base image:     ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.85.0
Morloc version: 0.85.0
Engine:         podman
SHM size:       512m
Dockerfile:     none
Flags:          /home/weena/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/env.flags.yaml
Data dir:       /home/weena/.local/share/morloc/environments/dnd
----

==== Step 2: Install modules and write a program

`morloc-manager run` executes a command inside an environment's container
(the default, or the one named with `--env`). Your current working directory is bind-mounted into the
container, so files you create on the host are visible inside, and vice
versa.

Use `--` to separate `morloc-manager` flags from inner command flags.
Without `--`, flags like `-o` or `--version` are consumed by
`morloc-manager run` itself rather than passed to the inner command.

Morloc's standard library is split into modules hosted on GitHub. Install
the ones this tutorial needs:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc install root root-py
----

Each `morloc install` fetches a module and places it in the environment's
`lib/` directory. `root` provides core type definitions; `root-py`
provides Python implementations of those types.

Create a new project directory, `dnd/`, and write the Morloc module:

[source, morloc]
.dnd.loc
----
module dnd (maximum, roll, rollAdv)

import root-py

source Py from "dice.py" ("roll")

--' Roll N dice with K pips per die
roll ::
  --' Number of dice to roll
  --' metavar: N
  Int ->
  --' Number of pips on each die
  --' metavar: K
  Int ->
  --' List of N dice roll results
  <Rand> [Int]

--' Get the maximum value in a list of integers
maximum :: [Int] -> Int
maximum = fold1 max

--' Roll 2d20 and take the higher result
rollAdv :: <Rand> Int
rollAdv = do
  xs <- roll 2 20
  maximum xs
----

The `<Rand>` tag in the return type is an effect annotation marking this
function as non-deterministic. It tells the Morloc type system that calling
`roll` may return different results each time.

And the Python implementation:

[source, python]
.dice.py
----
import random

def roll(n, d):
    return [random.randint(1, d) for _ in range(n)]
----

Build and run. The `--shell` flag drops you into an interactive shell
inside the container:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager shell
container$ morloc make dnd.loc
container$ ./dnd rollAdv
18
container$ ./dnd rollAdv
7
container$ exit
----

Or build and test in a single command:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- bash -c "morloc make dnd.loc && ./dnd rollAdv"
18
----

The compiled program is a self-contained CLI: each exported non-generic
term becomes a subcommand.

==== Step 3: Add cross-language functions

So far the program is pure Python. One of Morloc's distinctive features
is that you can swap a function's implementation to another language
without changing the composition. Let's implement `roll` in R and `max`
in {cpp}.

[source, r]
.dice.R
----
roll <- function(n, d) {
    sample(1:d, n, replace = TRUE)
}
----

[source, c++]
.dice.hpp
----
#pragma once
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

template <typename A>
A max(const std::vector<A>& xs) {
    return *std::max_element(xs.begin(), xs.end());
}
----

Update `dice.py` with a narration function:

[source, python]
.dice.py
----
import random

def roll(n, d):
    return [random.randint(1, d) for _ in range(n)]

def narrate(n):
    return f"You rolled a {n}"
----

Update `dnd.loc` to source from three languages:

[source, morloc]
.dnd.loc
----
module dnd (rollAdv)

import root-py
import root-cpp
import root-r

source R from "dice.R" ("roll")
source Cpp from "dice.hpp" ("max")
source Py from "dice.py" ("narrate")

roll :: Int -> Int -> <Rand> [Int]
max :: [Int] -> Int
narrate :: Int -> Str

rollAdv = do
  rolls <- roll 2 20
  narrate (max rolls)
----

Install the language-specific root modules:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc install root-r root-cpp
----

Build and test:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- bash -c "morloc make dnd.loc && ./dnd rollAdv"
"You rolled a 19"
----

The composition is unchanged. Morloc compiled a {cpp} pool, a Python
pool, and an R pool, and the nexus routes each call to the correct
runtime with automatic serialization between them.

==== Step 4: Package metadata

Every Morloc project needs a `package.yaml` that describes the package and
lists which files to bundle when installing. Initialize one with
`morloc new`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc new dnd
Created package.yaml for 'dnd'
----

NOTE: `morloc new` (inside the container) initializes a Morloc package --
it creates `package.yaml`. This is different from `morloc-manager new`
(on the host), which creates an environment.

By default, `morloc make --install` bundles all files in the project
directory (filtered by `.morlocignore`). For simple projects like this one,
that is sufficient -- you do not need to edit `package.yaml`.

For larger projects, you can explicitly list source files in the `include`
field to control what gets bundled:

[source, yaml]
.package.yaml
----
name: dnd
version: 0.1.0
synopsis: A dice roller demo
license: MIT
dependencies: []
include:
  - "dice.py"
  - "dice.R"
  - "dice.hpp"
----

The `include` field accepts exact file paths (`"dice.py"`), directories
with a trailing slash (`"src/"`), and glob patterns (`"*.py"`,
`"**/*.R"`). When `include` is specified:

* Every file you reference with `source ... from "path"` in a `.loc` file
  must be covered by at least one `include` entry. `morloc make --install`
  errors out if a directly sourced file is missing from `include`.
  Transitive dependencies (e.g., one R file calling `source()` on another)
  are _not_ auto-discovered, so you must list those yourself.
* All `include` paths are resolved relative to the directory that holds
  `package.yaml`. Absolute paths and `..` traversals are rejected --
  installs must stay self-contained within the project directory. The
  relative subdirectory layout is preserved on install (a file listed as
  `src/helpers/util.py` lands at `.../src/helpers/util.py`, not
  `.../util.py`).

==== Step 5: Add a system dependency

Real projects often need system packages beyond what the base Morloc image
provides. You can layer a custom Dockerfile on top of the base image
without leaving the `morloc-manager` interface.

Generate a stub Dockerfile:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update --env dnd --dockerfile-stub
Updating environment: dnd
Stub Dockerfile: ~/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/Dockerfile
Edit it, then run: morloc-manager update --env dnd
Environment 'dnd' updated.
----

Edit that file to install `jq` (a common JSON processing tool):

[source, dockerfile]
.~/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/Dockerfile
----
ARG CONTAINER_BASE=scratch
FROM ${CONTAINER_BASE}

RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y jq && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
----

The `CONTAINER_BASE` build arg is substituted with the environment's base
image at build time. Rebuild:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update --env dnd
Updating environment: dnd
...
Environment 'dnd' updated.
----

Verify the new tool is available inside the container:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- jq --version
jq-1.7.1
----

This same mechanism works for any system package, Python library, R
package, or arbitrary Dockerfile instruction. A content hash on the
Dockerfile lets `update` skip the rebuild when nothing has changed.

You can also include extra files in the build context using the `-i` flag:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update --env dnd --dockerfile ./Dockerfile -i ./requirements.txt
----

The `jq` package is just a stand-in here -- the dice roller doesn't need
it. In a real project you would add whatever your code actually depends on
(e.g., `libcurl-dev`, a Python package via `pip install`, or an R package
via `install.packages()`).

==== Step 6: Managing environments

As you work on more projects, you'll accumulate environments. Here is how
to manage them.

List all environments:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager ls
Local environments:
  dnd [0.85.0] (default)
----

Act on any environment with `--env`, and change which one is the default with
`modify --set-default`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager new experiments --morloc-version 0.85.0
$ morloc-manager run --env experiments -- morloc --version   # use it directly
$ morloc-manager modify --env experiments --set-default      # make it the default
$ morloc-manager modify --env dnd --set-default              # switch back
----

Show detailed info:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager info dnd
----

Update an existing environment. The `update` command accepts the same
flags as `new` but defaults to keeping existing values:

[%nowrap, console]
----
# Re-solve and rebuild (keeps the env's current morloc version)
$ morloc-manager update --env dnd

# Re-copy dotfiles after editing them
$ morloc-manager modify --env dnd --dotfiles ~/dotfiles

# Upgrade to a new morloc version (re-solves and rebuilds)
$ morloc-manager update --env dnd --morloc-version 0.80.0
----

Remove an environment you no longer need:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager rm experiments
Removed environment: experiments
----

==== Step 7: Using Apptainer / Singularity

The dnd walkthrough above works identically under Apptainer -- the
container-engine choice is hidden behind `morloc-manager run`. This
section covers the things that *do* differ: how environments are built,
where images live on disk, the Apptainer-specific build flags, and how
the host filesystem is shared with the container. If you only ever use
Docker or Podman, you can skip this section.

Apptainer support exists for two reasons. First, HPC clusters typically
have Apptainer (and no Docker daemon, no rootful Podman). Second,
Apptainer's image format (`.sif`) is a single file you can copy to a
shared filesystem and `exec` from every compute node -- a natural fit
for SLURM-style job dispatch.

`apptainer` and `singularity` are treated as the same engine throughout.
Wherever this tutorial writes `apptainer`, you may write `singularity`.

===== Selecting the engine

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager new --engine apptainer myenv
----

If only `apptainer` (or `singularity`) is on `PATH`, `new` auto-detects
it. The runtime binary is resolved at use time, so an env created with
`--engine apptainer` runs equally well on a host that only has
`singularity`.

===== Pulling the base image

`morloc-manager new` under Apptainer pulls the same GHCR OCI image used
by the Docker/Podman engines and converts it to a local `.sif`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager new dnd
Pulling docker://ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.85.0...
Created environment: dnd
Initializing morloc (this may take several minutes)...
Environment 'dnd' is ready.
----

The base `.sif` lives at `<env-data-dir>/sif/base.sif` and is
morloc-owned; removing the env removes the `.sif`. The first pull is
slow (OCI -> `.sif` conversion takes 30-60 s for `morloc-full`);
subsequent runs are fast.

`morloc-manager info dnd` shows the Apptainer-specific fields:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager info dnd
Name:           dnd
Scope:          local
Default:        yes
Base image:     ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.85.0
Morloc version: 0.85.0
Engine:         apptainer
Base SIF:       ~/.local/share/morloc/environments/dnd/sif/base.sif
Layered SIF:    (none)
Def file:       none
Flags:          ~/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/env.flags.yaml
Data dir:       ~/.local/share/morloc/environments/dnd
----

The SHM-size field is omitted under Apptainer -- the container shares
the host's `/dev/shm`, so morloc's shared-memory IPC just works at
whatever size the host provides.

===== Native build recipes: `.def` files

Apptainer cannot read Dockerfiles. The equivalent recipe format is a
Singularity definition file (`.def`). Generate a stub:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update --env dnd --deffile-stub
Updating environment: dnd
Stub def file: ~/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/recipe.def
Edit it, then run: morloc-manager update --env dnd
----

The stub looks like this:

[source]
.~/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/recipe.def
----
Bootstrap: localimage
From: {{ BASE_SIF }}

%post
    set -euo pipefail

    # apt-get on hosts without a full subuid range needs APT::Sandbox::User=root:
    #   apt-get -o APT::Sandbox::User=root update \
    #     && apt-get -o APT::Sandbox::User=root install -y jq \
    #     && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
    :
----

`{{ BASE_SIF }}` is substituted with the path to the env's `base.sif`
at build time via Apptainer's own `--build-arg` mechanism. `set -euo
pipefail` makes any failure in `%post` break the build instead of
silently passing. Uncomment the apt example (or write your own), then
rebuild:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update --env dnd
Updating environment: dnd
INFO:    Build mode set explicitly: ...
...
Environment 'dnd' updated.
----

No Docker daemon, no rootful Podman, no `sudo` is required. The result
is `<env-data-dir>/sif/layered.sif`, which `morloc-manager run`
automatically prefers over `base.sif`.

morloc-manager passes no mode-selecting flags to `apptainer build` by
default -- Apptainer's own behavior applies, and its own error messages
surface unmodified if something goes wrong. On HPC-style hosts where
`/etc/subuid` is mapped but `newuidmap` is not setuid-root, Apptainer
will FATAL with a hint about `--ignore-subuid`; apply it either in
`env.flags.yaml` under `build.apptainer` (persistent) or one-shot via
`morloc-manager update --reinit --reinit-arg --ignore-subuid`.

If both a `Dockerfile` and a `recipe.def` exist in the env config
directory, Apptainer prefers the `.def` (engine-native). If only a
`Dockerfile` is present and Docker or Podman is also installed,
morloc-manager falls back to building the OCI image and then converting
it to `.sif` -- convenient for cross-engine development but unavailable
on pure-Apptainer HPC hosts.

===== Host home and the `MORLOC_BIN_LINK_DIR` env var

Apptainer's design philosophy is the opposite of Docker's: the
container inherits the host environment by default. Your `$HOME` is
bind-mounted into the container, your shell environment is inherited,
and the calling UID is the in-container UID. This is what HPC users
expect -- you can read your own files inside the container without
mounting flags.

That openness creates one wrinkle. `morloc init` (which runs inside the
container during `new` and `update --reinit`) symlinks the `morloc` and
`morloc-nexus` binaries into a `PATH` directory. The Docker/Podman
default of `~/.local/bin` would clobber the host's `~/.local/bin`
because home is shared. morloc-manager handles this by exporting
`MORLOC_BIN_LINK_DIR=$HOME/.local/share/morloc/bin` to the container
and prepending that path to `PATH`. Inside the container, `morloc` and
`morloc-nexus` resolve there; on the host, your own `~/.local/bin` is
untouched.

You can override the target directory by adding
`MORLOC_BIN_LINK_DIR=...` to `env.flags.yaml` under `run.apptainer` as
a `--env KEY=VALUE` flag, or one-shot via `morloc-manager run -e
MORLOC_BIN_LINK_DIR=/some/dir -- morloc init -f`. Setting it to the
empty string disables the symlinks entirely (in that case `morloc
init` uses the binaries from their installed locations under
`/opt/morloc/bin`, which is already on `PATH` inside the container).

===== Layered SIF and the `dnd` walkthrough

The dnd dice roller from Steps 1-5 works identically under Apptainer.
The only visible differences are the build output (Apptainer instead of
Buildah/BuildKit) and `info`'s fields. After Step 5 finishes you should
see a `Layered SIF` entry pointing at `<env-data-dir>/sif/layered.sif`,
and `morloc-manager run -- jq --version` should print the jq version
from inside the layered `.sif`.

===== When not to use Apptainer

On a local Linux workstation with no HPC ambitions, Podman is still the
recommended engine -- it is more deeply tested, has faster build
iteration, and avoids the OCI-to-`.sif` conversion latency. The
Apptainer engine is for HPC, shared filesystems, and SLURM dispatch.
Cross-environment portability is not a problem in practice: an env's
`.loc` source files and its `package.yaml` are engine-agnostic, so a
project you develop under Podman ships to an Apptainer host with no
changes.

==== Step 8: SLURM dispatch with `--slurm-bridge`

A morloc program normally executes inside the driver container the
nexus was started in. Labeled function calls let you redirect specific
calls to SLURM-managed compute nodes, where they run inside the
**same** container image as the driver. The mechanism is a UDS bridge
that morloc-manager exposes to the container, plus the host's
`sbatch`/`sacct` clients (which the container itself does not need).

The default engine for SLURM dispatch is Apptainer because its `.sif`
files live on the shared filesystem and are reachable on every compute
node without extra image distribution. Podman and Docker work too,
provided you can get the env's image onto every compute node
(registry pull or a pre-populated image store). morloc-manager will
warn but not refuse to start the bridge under those engines.

Before relying on this in production, run the doctor check:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager doctor --slurm
----

This validates the prerequisites discussed below
(`sbatch`/`sacct` reachable, `slurm-support: true` in the build
config, an env image present, morloc-manager binary at an
NFS-shareable path, runtime dir writable).

===== Source-level: label a call

In a `.loc` file, prefix a call with `LABEL:`:

[source, morloc]
.heavy.loc
----
module heavy (foo)

import root-py

source Py from "myjob.py" ("myjob")

myjob :: Int -> Int

foo :: Int -> Int
foo x = big:myjob x
----

The label `big` is a free-form name that maps to a SLURM resource spec
in a sidecar YAML next to the `.loc` file:

[source, yaml]
.heavy.yaml
----
labeled-groups:
  big:
    cache: false
    benchmark: false
    remote:
      threads: 8
      memory: 32        # GB
      time: 3600        # seconds
      gpus: 0
----

The presence of `remote:` is what activates SLURM dispatch for that
call. Without it the label still defines a config group (useful for
the cache/benchmark fields, coming later) but the call runs locally.

===== Build-time: enable RemoteCall codegen

The compiler only emits SLURM-style remote calls when the build
config says so. The easiest one-time setup is to pass `--slurm`
through `morloc-manager update`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update --reinit --init-arg --slurm
----

`--init-arg <arg>` is a generic pass-through to `morloc init -f` --
any compiler init flag works without a new morloc-manager flag. The
equivalent inside the container is `morloc init -f --slurm`. Either
way the effect is writing `slurm-support: true` to
`~/.config/morloc/build.yaml`; every subsequent `morloc make` then
emits the right code for labeled-`remote:` calls.

The same pass-through works at env creation:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager new hpcdemo --engine apptainer --init-arg --slurm
----

===== Runtime: --slurm-bridge

Run the program through morloc-manager with `--slurm-bridge`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run --slurm-bridge -- bash -c "morloc make heavy.loc && ./heavy foo 5"
----

What happens under the hood:

1. morloc-manager resolves the active env, confirms an image is
   reachable, and spawns a bridge listener on
   `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/morloc-bridge-<pid>.sock`.
2. The driver container is launched with the socket bind-mounted at
   `/run/morloc-bridge.sock` and
   `MORLOC_BRIDGE_SOCKET=/run/morloc-bridge.sock` exported.
3. The driver nexus inside the container exports
   `MORLOC_NEXUS_PATH=<absolute nexus binary path>` to every pool it
   spawns. Apptainer auto-mounts `$HOME`, so the same absolute path is
   reachable on every compute node (assuming an NFS-mounted home --
   the HPC norm).
4. The pool reaches `big:myjob 5`, calls `libmorloc::remote_call`,
   which detects `MORLOC_BRIDGE_SOCKET` and sends a JSON `submit` RPC
   carrying the nexus invocation as a structured `inner_argv` array.
5. The bridge composes the full argv
   `[morloc-manager, run, --slurm-bridge, --] ++ inner_argv`,
   shell-escapes it once, and submits via
   `sbatch --parsable -o ... --mem=... --time=... --wrap='...'`,
   returning the slurm job id. The bridge never talks to Apptainer
   directly -- the compute-node `morloc-manager run` does that, using
   the same env config the driver started from (which is on
   NFS-shared `$HOME`).
6. SLURM dispatches the wrap to a compute node. The compute-node shell
   runs `morloc-manager run --slurm-bridge -- <nexus> --call-packet
   ...`, which brings up the same env's container (same image, same
   `/opt/morloc` mount, same `$HOME` propagation), spawns its OWN
   local bridge UDS, then execs the in-container nexus in
   `--call-packet` mode. That nexus starts its own pools, sends the
   call packet to the targeted pool, writes the result packet to the
   shared `.morloc-cache/` directory, and exits. Because the
   compute-side morloc-manager also enabled `--slurm-bridge`, the
   in-container pool can itself fire nested labeled calls back to
   slurm with no extra wiring (Phase 7 of the architecture).
7. The driver runtime polls `sacct -j ID` (via the bridge) until
   COMPLETED, reads the result packet from the cache, validates the
   packet header, and returns the bytes up through the pool to the
   nexus.

The driver container never invokes `sbatch` or `sacct` directly. The
compute-node container also never does -- it talks to its own host's
sbatch via its OWN bridge. The bridge UDS is the sole boundary
between containerized morloc and the host's SLURM tools at each
level.

NOTE: For step 6 to succeed, the morloc-manager binary on the driver
host must be reachable at the same absolute path on every compute
node. The typical setup is `~/.local/bin/morloc-manager` on
NFS-mounted `$HOME`. Verify with `ssh <compute-node> ls -l
$(which morloc-manager)` before relying on this in production; the
`doctor --slurm` check above already warns when this looks wrong.

===== Path-mirroring assumption

The default flow assumes the user's `$HOME` is an NFS-mounted
filesystem visible at the same absolute path on every compute node.
If your cluster mounts shared storage at a different path (`/scratch`,
`/data/<user>`), add a matching `apptainer exec --bind` to the env's
`env.flags`:

[source, yaml]
.~/.config/morloc/environments/myenv/env.flags.yaml
----
run:
  apptainer:
    - "--bind"
    - "/scratch:/scratch"
----

Whatever paths the morloc program writes to (cache, packets, the nexus
binary) must resolve to the same files on the compute node. Build the
program in a directory under those paths and the rest is automatic.

===== What lands in `.morloc-cache/`

Each remote call creates a small family of content-addressed files in
the project's `.morloc-cache/` directory. Knowing the layout makes the
cache easy to inspect or to clean by hand.

|===
| File | Written by | Purpose

| `<arg-hash>.dat`
| driver
| Raw msgpack payload of one argument. Content-addressed by its xxhash --
  passing the same value twice (e.g. a 10 GB genome) reuses the same file.

| `<arg-hash>.packet`
| driver
| Small morloc packet header (`FILE+MSGPACK`) whose payload IS the path
  to the sibling `.dat`. The driver's call-packet builder concatenates
  these tiny header packets, NOT the bulky `.dat` bytes.

| `<call-hash>-call.dat`
| driver
| The assembled call packet: a `CALL` header + the concatenated
  `.packet` headers. Tiny -- bounded by the number of args and the
  length of each argument's path, never by the data volume.

| `<call-hash>.dat`
| compute-node nexus
| The result packet. The driver pool reads this after the slurm job
  completes; the same file doubles as the memoization entry for any
  later call with identical args.

| `<call-hash>.out` / `.err`
| sbatch
| Stdout/stderr of the slurm job. Empty on success; useful when a
  job fails and the result packet validity check rejects the output.
|===

Two consequences: large data flows by reference, not by value (the
`-call.dat` stays small even when the arg is huge), and a re-run with
unchanged args hits the memoized result without resubmitting to slurm.
The cache currently grows monotonically; a `morloc-manager clean` is
on the roadmap.

==== What's next

At this point you can create environments, install modules, write
cross-language morloc programs, add system dependencies, manage
multiple environments, and dispatch labeled calls to SLURM-managed
compute nodes.

link:tutorial-deployment.asc[Part 2] picks up from here and covers
installing programs permanently, serving them over HTTP, exporting
portable images, system-wide environments, and health checks with
`doctor`.
